# gscope — Generic Text Search Mode for cscope

## Overview

gscope is a language-agnostic extension of the classic cscope source code
navigation tool. While cscope is purpose-built for C/C++ source navigation,
gscope repurposes cscope's proven cross-reference engine for searching any
text-based file corpus — markdown documents, plain text, shell scripts,
configuration files, novel chapters, wiki pages, and more.

gscope is not a separate program. It is the same cscope binary invoked via
a symlink named 'gscope'. When cscope detects that it was invoked as 'gscope'
via argv[0], it automatically activates generic search mode.

Similarly, gscope-indexer and cscope-indexer are the same script — the
indexer detects its invocation name via argv[0] and selects appropriate
default file extensions automatically.

## Key Differences from cscope

| Feature                  | cscope              | gscope                        |
|--------------------------|---------------------|-------------------------------|
| Search fields            | 10 C-specific       | 1 generic                     |
| Case sensitivity         | Configurable        | Always case-insensitive       |
| Boolean expressions      | No                  | Yes (&&, \|\|, (( )))         |
| File types               | C/C++ source        | Any text file                 |
| Screen real estate       | 10 field lines      | 1 field line                  |
| cscope compatibility     | N/A                 | Full — same binary            |

## Installation

```bash
# Build from source
cd cscope
./configure
make

# Create the gscope symlink
cd src
ln -sf cscope gscope

# Create the cscope-indexer symlink
ln -sf gscope-indexer cscope-indexer

# Install binaries and scripts
cp cscope gscope ~/bin/
cp gscope-indexer cscope-indexer ~/bin/
# or system-wide:
sudo cp cscope gscope /usr/local/bin/
sudo cp gscope-indexer cscope-indexer /usr/local/bin/
```

## Indexing Your Files

Both `gscope-indexer` and `cscope-indexer` are the same script. The script
detects its invocation name and selects default extensions accordingly:

| Invocation        | Default extensions                                  |
|-------------------|-----------------------------------------------------|
| `gscope-indexer`  | md txt cap wiki rst adoc html xml json yaml yml     |
|                   | sh pl py rb lua go                                  |
| `cscope-indexer`  | c h l y cc hh cpp hpp cxx                           |

In either mode, the `-e` option overrides the default extension list.

### gscope-indexer Examples

```bash
# Index markdown and cap files recursively (default gscope extensions)
gscope-indexer -r .

# Index specific extensions
gscope-indexer -r -e "md txt cap" .

# Verbose output with custom database name
gscope-indexer -v -r -e "md cap" -f myproject.out .

# Build file list only (no database)
gscope-indexer -l -r -e "md txt" .
```

### cscope-indexer Examples

```bash
# Index C/C++ source files recursively (default cscope extensions)
cscope-indexer -r .

# Index with verbose output
cscope-indexer -v -r .

# Override extensions
cscope-indexer -r -e "c h cpp hpp" .
```

### Indexer Options

| Option          | Description                                        |
|-----------------|----------------------------------------------------|
| `-e "ext1 ext2"`| Space-separated list of extensions to index        |
| `-f file`       | Database output file (default: cscope.out)         |
| `-i file`       | File list output file (default: cscope.files)      |
| `-r`            | Recurse into subdirectories                        |
| `-l`            | List files only, do not build database             |
| `-v`            | Verbose progress output                            |

## Launching gscope

```bash
# Launch in current directory (auto-indexes if needed)
gscope

# Launch with recursion
gscope -R

# Launch against a named database
gscope -f myproject.out

# Launch against a named database with recursion
gscope -R -f myproject.out
```

## Search Expression Syntax

gscope supports a boolean expression syntax for powerful multi-term searches.
All matching is case-insensitive regardless of input case.

### Simple Search

```
leviathan
```
Finds all lines containing the substring "leviathan".

### AND Search ( && )

```
dave&&barry
```
Finds lines containing BOTH "dave" AND "barry".

```
marc&&lexi&&parkour
```
Finds lines containing ALL THREE substrings.

### OR Search ( || )

```
dave||david
```
Finds lines containing EITHER "dave" OR "david".

### Grouped Boolean Expressions ( (( )) )

Use `((` and `))` to group OR alternatives, connected by `&&`.
Groups are evaluated recursively, so `&&` works inside groups too:

```
((dave||david))&&((barry||barry jr))
```
Finds lines where (dave OR david) AND (barry OR barry jr) are both present.

```
((marc||marcus))&&stewart
```
Finds lines containing any variant of the first name AND "stewart".

```
((died||death||fallen||killed))&&maya
```
Finds lines referencing maya in the context of death or injury.

```
((wing chun||hung gar||white crane))&&((strike||kick||block))
```
Finds combat lines referencing specific martial arts styles.

```
((str1&&str2||str3))
```
Finds lines where (str1 AND str2) OR str3 is present — && works
inside groups, with || separating alternatives.

### Expression Evaluation Rules

1. Top-level `&&` splits the expression into AND operands, respecting
   `(( ))` group boundaries — `&&` inside a group is NOT a top-level split
2. Each AND operand is evaluated as:
   - A `(( ))` group: content split on `||`, each alternative evaluated
     recursively (enabling `&&` inside groups)
   - A plain term with `||`: evaluated as OR across alternatives
   - A plain substring: direct case-insensitive match
3. All AND operands must match for the line to be returned
4. All matching is case-insensitive regardless of input case
5. Empty terms (e.g. leading/trailing `&&`) are silently ignored

## Keyboard Navigation

gscope uses the same keyboard interface as cscope:

| Key          | Action                                      |
|--------------|---------------------------------------------|
| Enter        | Execute search                              |
| Tab          | Move between results and search field       |
| 0-9 / a-z    | Jump directly to a result line              |
| Space        | Next page of results                        |
| +/-          | Scroll results forward/backward             |
| Ctrl-D       | Page down through results                   |
| Ctrl-U       | Page up through results                     |
| Ctrl-E       | Edit file at result line                    |
| ?            | Help screen                                 |
| q            | Quit                                        |

## Typical Workflows

### Novel / Long-form Writing

```bash
cd ~/projects/MyNovel
gscope-indexer -v -r -e "md txt" .
gscope

# Search examples:
# maya                              find all Maya references
# maya&&chapter                     Maya references within chapter context
# ((died||death||fallen))&&maya     Maya death/injury references
# ((marc||lexi))&&((marc||lexi))    lines where both characters appear
```

### Configuration and Skills Files

```bash
cd ~/fabrique/skills
gscope-indexer -v -r -e "cap xml" .
gscope -R

# Search examples:
# ((article||chapter))&&size        size definitions in authoring skills
# ((markdown||html))&&output        output format references
```

### C/C++ Source (classic cscope behavior)

```bash
cd ~/projects/myproject
cscope-indexer -v -r .
cscope -R
```

### Mixed Project (Code + Docs)

```bash
gscope-indexer -v -r -e "md txt sh py yaml c h" .
gscope -R
```

## Technical Notes

- gscope and cscope are the same binary; behavior is selected via argv[0]
- gscope-indexer and cscope-indexer are the same script; default extensions
  are selected via argv[0]
- gscope searches perform direct line-by-line file scanning, bypassing the
  cscope cross-reference index. This is identical to how cscope itself
  handles text string searches (field 4) — no performance penalty.
- cscope.out is used by gscope as a file manifest (list of files to search);
  the C-language cross-reference index within it is not consulted.
- Performance scales linearly with total corpus size. For typical project
  sizes (hundreds of files, tens of thousands of lines) searches complete
  in well under one second.

## Version

This repository is based on cscope 15.8a. The gscope extensions are
versioned as 15.8a-gscope1.

## Origin

gscope was developed as a fork of the cscope 15.8a codebase maintained
by Laurent Boulard (https://github.com/lboulard/cscope).

cscope was originally developed at Bell Labs in the 1970s and has been
a staple of Unix C development environments for over 50 years. gscope
extends that legacy by removing the C-language constraint, making
cscope's proven navigation engine broadly applicable to any text corpus.

Fork maintained at: https://github.com/theduke460/cscope
